            <ArticleTitle>Efficacy of limited-duration spinal cord stimulation for subacute postherpetic neuralgia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1004-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Excellent outcomes were achieved with spinal cord stimulation (SCS) for 7 to 10 days on 2 patients who developed postherpetic neuralgia. Both patients were within 2 to 3 months of the onset of the condition, and nerve blocks provided only temporary pain relief and drug therapies had poor efficacy. The authors believe that limited-duration SCS for subacute postherpetic neuralgia is a useful treatment approach that may prevent the pain from progressing to chronic postherpetic neuralgia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
